\b How is this organized?\

\b0 All end-to-end tests go under \'91src/tests/e2e\'92. All unit tests go under \'91src/tests/unit-tests\'92. \
\

\b How do I write a test?\

\b0 Go to the \'91spec.ts\'92 file for the page you want to write a test for. For example, \'91home.spec.ts\'92 runs the tests for the home page. \
\

\b What are the .ts files in \'91src/tests/page-objects\'92 for?\

\b0 Page Objects contain all of the variables containing the elements of that page, and general actions for that page. For example, \'91home-page.ts\'92 contains public variables for the elements and public methods for general actions on the homepage. 
\i There should be no \'91expect\'92 or \'91browser.wait\'92 statements in Page Objects. 
\i0 Import a page object at the beginning of a \'91~spec.ts\'92 to use it.\
\

\b What is up with the PageObjectInterface that\'92s being implemented?\

\b0 Use this so there are certain functions that are on 
\i every 
\i0 page object. For example, \'91void getPage()\'92 is a good one to keep in there because we should be able to import a Page Object and get the url of the page that is referring to.\
\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

